WEBVTT

00:03.960 --> 00:06.120
So now what is exactly a cross-join?

00:06.160 --> 00:11.880
Now, in some scenarios, we want to combine every row from the left, every row from the right.

00:11.880 --> 00:16.360
So that means I want to see all the possible combinations from both tables.

00:16.360 --> 00:19.280
So we are doing something called like Cartesian join.

00:19.280 --> 00:24.880
So now if we look to our two circles we want everything from a and as well everything from B.

00:24.880 --> 00:29.640
So that means I want to see everything from A combined with everything with b.

00:29.680 --> 00:33.080
So in this example we have two rows in A and three rows in B.

00:33.120 --> 00:38.640
If we do a cross join you will get six possible combinations by just multiplying the number of rows

00:38.680 --> 00:39.720
between A and B.

00:39.720 --> 00:41.560
So be careful using the cross join.

00:41.560 --> 00:46.360
If you use it, you will get like crazy number of rows in the results and you're going to make the database

00:46.360 --> 00:48.480
really busy finding out the results for you.

00:48.480 --> 00:49.520
So now about the syntax.

00:49.520 --> 00:50.960
It's going to be the easiest.

00:50.960 --> 00:54.480
So you start as usual from one of those tables the A for example.

00:54.480 --> 00:56.400
And then you say cross join B.

00:56.440 --> 01:00.600
So now my friends, if you look at this, you can see it's not like the previous joins that we have

01:00.600 --> 01:00.800
done.

01:00.800 --> 01:05.120
We have always before talked about unmatching rows, matching rows and so on.

01:05.120 --> 01:08.790
But here we don't care at all about whether the data is matching or not.

01:08.830 --> 01:11.950
I just want to see all the possible combinations, everything.

01:11.950 --> 01:17.150
So since we don't care about matching the two tables, we don't have to specify any condition.

01:17.150 --> 01:21.430
So there is no need to use the keyword on because we don't need any condition.

01:21.430 --> 01:22.110
So that's it.

01:22.110 --> 01:24.830
You just say cross join B and the magic can happen.

01:24.830 --> 01:26.110
So this is the cross join.

01:26.110 --> 01:28.190
Let's go to SQL to try that okay.

01:28.230 --> 01:29.390
So now we have the following task.

01:29.430 --> 01:34.310
It says generate all possible combinations of customers and orders.

01:34.310 --> 01:37.830
So that means we want everything with everything using the cross join.

01:37.830 --> 01:39.030
And this can be very simple.

01:39.030 --> 01:42.230
So we're going to start with select star from whatever table.

01:42.230 --> 01:43.950
So you can start from the customers.

01:43.950 --> 01:47.630
And then you say cross join orders.

01:47.830 --> 01:48.430
That's it.

01:48.470 --> 01:49.070
Very simple.

01:49.070 --> 01:50.270
Let's go and execute it.

01:50.270 --> 01:52.950
So now as you know we have five customers and four orders.

01:52.950 --> 01:56.550
And if you multiply them you will get in the results 20 rows.

01:56.550 --> 02:01.390
So now we are getting everything with everything even if the data is not matching at all.

02:01.390 --> 02:03.870
So you can see for example the orders here.

02:03.870 --> 02:08.030
So this is one order that belongs only to one customer, the customer ID one.

02:08.030 --> 02:13.980
So it is an order from actually Maria, but still we are seeing this same order with the other customers

02:13.980 --> 02:17.780
since we want to combine everything with everything, so there are no rules.

02:17.780 --> 02:19.380
The same thing for the next sets.

02:19.420 --> 02:22.220
So this is the second order actually belongs to John.

02:22.220 --> 02:24.460
But we are seeing this order with all customers.

02:24.460 --> 02:25.460
So that's it.

02:25.460 --> 02:27.500
This is how the cross join works.

02:27.500 --> 02:29.260
And now you might ask me why we have this.

02:29.300 --> 02:30.500
It makes no sense, right?

02:30.500 --> 02:32.380
Well my friends, I rarely use it.

02:32.380 --> 02:37.660
But sometimes if I want to generate like test data or maybe if you have like for example, a table called

02:37.660 --> 02:42.820
colors and a table called products, and you would like to see all the combinations between the products

02:42.820 --> 02:43.740
and the colors.

02:43.740 --> 02:48.980
So in some scenarios, it makes really sense to see all your products together with all the colors without

02:48.980 --> 02:50.980
any matching conditions or whatever.

02:50.980 --> 02:56.060
So there are like few scenarios for the cross join if you are like doing simulations or testing.

02:56.060 --> 02:58.700
So this is how we do the cross join.

02:58.740 --> 03:00.420
Okay, so that's all about the cross join.

03:00.420 --> 03:03.900
And with that we have covered the four advanced types of joins.

03:03.940 --> 03:08.260
Now if you look at this you might ask okay how am I going to choose between all those types.

03:08.260 --> 03:10.540
So you might ask me okay how you do it.

03:10.540 --> 03:15.500
Well I'm going to show you now my decision tree that I usually follow in order to choose the correct

03:15.540 --> 03:15.900
type.
